WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.334
This is the executive briefing
for Book 14 of the Federal

3
00:00:04.334 --> 00:00:08.437
Modernization Workbook, Federal
Compliance and Authorization.

4
00:00:08.437 --> 00:00:14.364
It's for the people who sign for a system
or answer for it: authorizing officials,

5
00:00:14.364 --> 00:00:19.288
the CISO and CIO, ISSOs, the privacy
officer and program managers.

6
00:00:19.288 --> 00:00:21.638
The book's argument fits in one line.

7
00:00:21.638 --> 00:00:25.577
A control described in a plan but
never shown working is a promise,

8
00:00:25.577 --> 00:00:30.174
and an authorization built on promises
decays the day after the assessment.

9
00:00:30.174 --> 00:00:34.770
I'll take four questions: why every
control needs proof, what each control

10
00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:39.212
family needs, who owns compliance,
and how to build and keep the package.

11
00:00:39.212 --> 00:00:43.612
Each decision slide names the decision,
its owner, and when it's needed.

12
00:00:43.612 --> 00:00:46.120
Let's start with who owes which proof.

13
00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:54.583
An authorizing official doesn't need to
know a control is supposed to be in place.

14
00:00:54.583 --> 00:00:57.545
They need to know it is,
today, and how they know.

15
00:00:57.545 --> 00:01:00.214
A promise is a statement
in the security plan,

16
00:01:00.214 --> 00:01:02.883
backed by evidence
someone gathered by hand.

17
00:01:02.883 --> 00:01:05.194
It's been aging since
the day it was signed.

18
00:01:05.194 --> 00:01:07.347
Proof is a record the system produces,

19
00:01:07.347 --> 00:01:10.958
which another reviewer can check
again and get the same answer.

20
00:01:10.958 --> 00:01:13.532
The proof is owed by
different parties, too.

21
00:01:13.532 --> 00:01:17.819
The provider proves its service;
the agency proves its own system.

22
00:01:17.819 --> 00:01:21.859
So the decision for the authorizing
official: reuse the provider's

23
00:01:21.859 --> 00:01:26.558
certification as evidence, and authorize
the agency's own system that uses it.

24
00:01:26.558 --> 00:01:29.340
Next, what Moderate commits you to.

25
00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:36.465
Most civilian cloud systems
are authorized at Moderate,

26
00:01:36.465 --> 00:01:38.978
and the word is a
commitment of a known size.

27
00:01:38.978 --> 00:01:42.134
The agency rates each kind
of information it holds;

28
00:01:42.134 --> 00:01:45.741
one moderate rating and none
high makes a moderate system.

29
00:01:45.741 --> 00:01:50.798
Then it tailors NIST's Moderate
baseline: 287 controls and enhancements,

30
00:01:50.798 --> 00:01:55.603
every one of which the authorizing
official accepts on the evidence.

31
00:01:55.603 --> 00:01:58.339
FedRAMP's Class C is a separate fact.

32
00:01:58.339 --> 00:02:03.340
It lists 322, and 36 of them
sit outside NIST Moderate.

33
00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:05.078
So cite the two separately.

34
00:02:05.078 --> 00:02:07.512
The decision: the authorizing official,

35
00:02:07.512 --> 00:02:11.936
with the privacy official for any
system holding personal information,

36
00:02:11.936 --> 00:02:16.066
approves each categorization before
a single control is selected.

37
00:02:16.066 --> 00:02:19.060
Next, why annual is only the floor.

38
00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:26.223
Some programs still plan around
a three-year authorization.

39
00:02:26.223 --> 00:02:27.510
There's no such rule.

40
00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:32.271
FISMA requires testing at least
annually, and OMB's Circular A-130

41
00:02:32.271 --> 00:02:37.297
points agencies to ongoing
authorization once a system is eligible.

42
00:02:37.297 --> 00:02:39.169
Only proof can keep up with that,

43
00:02:39.169 --> 00:02:43.272
because only proof can be regenerated
without rebuilding it by hand.

44
00:02:43.272 --> 00:02:45.734
It's also why assessment tools come last.

45
00:02:45.734 --> 00:02:50.785
A tool like ScubaGear measures drift from
an intended design; it can't author one.

46
00:02:50.785 --> 00:02:53.287
Run first, it hands
you an accurate list of

47
00:02:53.287 --> 00:02:56.010
findings and nothing
that keeps them closed.

48
00:02:56.010 --> 00:03:01.094
So the decision for the CIO and executive
sponsor: fund the mechanisms that produce

49
00:03:01.094 --> 00:03:05.810
records before the tools that measure
them, and run the tools as confirmation.

50
00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:08.060
Next, the federal clock.

51
00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:15.106
The dates that drive this
work belong to FedRAMP,

52
00:03:15.106 --> 00:03:18.485
CISA and OMB, and each one
binds a different party.

53
00:03:18.485 --> 00:03:20.693
As the book checked on October 21,

54
00:03:20.693 --> 00:03:24.210
the post-quantum migration
plan was due the next day.

55
00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:27.510
The Agency Logging Plan
falls on November 18.

56
00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:32.597
On December 7, BOD 26-04's
remediation timelines bind agencies,

57
00:03:32.597 --> 00:03:36.985
while FedRAMP's vulnerability
rules bind providers.

58
00:03:36.985 --> 00:03:41.860
And FedRAMP's 2026 rules
become mandatory on January 1.

59
00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:45.910
And dates move: several in the
program's own sources had drifted.

60
00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:51.059
So the decision: keep one dated register,
with the source, the exact wording,

61
00:03:51.059 --> 00:03:56.123
whom it binds, and the day it was last
checked, and re-verify it at every gate.

62
00:03:56.123 --> 00:03:58.484
A date in a package is evidence.

63
00:03:58.484 --> 00:04:01.400
Next, what the directives leave behind.

64
00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:08.975
Most directive deadlines have passed,
but their requirements haven't.

65
00:04:08.975 --> 00:04:15.025
BOD 26-04 replaced the old remediation
directives with timelines set by risk,

66
00:04:15.025 --> 00:04:18.050
and its third phase lands on December 7.

67
00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:22.366
BOD 25-01's dates are done,
yet every new cloud tenant

68
00:04:22.366 --> 00:04:26.275
still needs its baselines in
place before it's authorized.

69
00:04:26.275 --> 00:04:31.562
And a job that must run every seven days
is only proven by records showing it did.

70
00:04:31.562 --> 00:04:35.734
Vendor support dates become compliance
dates too: through the baseline,

71
00:04:35.734 --> 00:04:39.349
through directives like the one
on end-of-support edge devices,

72
00:04:39.349 --> 00:04:41.574
and through the authorization itself.

73
00:04:41.574 --> 00:04:46.825
So the decision for the authorizing
official: retire each unsupported component

74
00:04:46.825 --> 00:04:51.225
by its date, or explicitly accept the
risk of keeping it, as an open item.

75
00:04:51.225 --> 00:04:53.660
Next, what each control needs.

76
00:04:57.410 --> 00:05:00.385
“Is this required at Moderate?”
sounds like one question.

77
00:05:00.385 --> 00:05:03.410
It's two: NIST's baseline
for the agency's system,

78
00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:06.435
and FedRAMP's class for
the provider's service.

79
00:05:06.435 --> 00:05:09.665
The series crosswalk indexes 149 controls,

80
00:05:09.665 --> 00:05:15.497
but it's a pointer to where each one is
treated, not proof it's closed or inherited.

81
00:05:15.497 --> 00:05:18.429
It covers fewer than half
the Moderate baseline,

82
00:05:18.429 --> 00:05:20.847
so it can't stand in
for the security plan.

83
00:05:20.847 --> 00:05:25.920
The decision: fill the product inventory
questionnaire against the estate as it is,

84
00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:29.008
verify each FedRAMP status
on the day you record it,

85
00:05:29.008 --> 00:05:32.536
and decide every flag before
the security plan is written.

86
00:05:32.536 --> 00:05:37.360
An uncertified service inside the boundary
goes to the authorizing official.

87
00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:40.040
Next, when a control is closed.

88
00:05:43.790 --> 00:05:45.215
Is a control closed?

89
00:05:45.215 --> 00:05:47.336
Too often the answer is that the product

90
00:05:47.336 --> 00:05:49.715
was bought or the ticket
was marked complete.

91
00:05:49.715 --> 00:05:51.915
None of those is the control operating.

92
00:05:51.915 --> 00:05:53.778
The book applies two tests.

93
00:05:53.778 --> 00:05:57.327
The mechanism exists and
reaches what the control covers.

94
00:05:57.327 --> 00:06:00.763
And its proof is recorded:
a machine-readable check,

95
00:06:00.763 --> 00:06:02.890
a time, and what did the checking.

96
00:06:02.890 --> 00:06:07.533
A control that passes the first and
fails the second is worse than open:

97
00:06:07.533 --> 00:06:10.015
it looks closed and can't be shown to be.

98
00:06:10.015 --> 00:06:15.153
And a vendor roadmap isn't a commitment;
date every claim and confirm it first.

99
00:06:15.153 --> 00:06:18.959
The decision: count a control
closed only on both tests,

100
00:06:18.959 --> 00:06:22.003
and keep roadmap items
in the plan of action.

101
00:06:22.003 --> 00:06:24.700
Next, what no provider can close.

102
00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:31.988
Some control families no cloud
provider can close for you,

103
00:06:31.988 --> 00:06:34.200
and program management is the first.

104
00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:37.395
NIST marks the whole
family organization-wide,

105
00:06:37.395 --> 00:06:41.737
outside every system baseline,
and FedRAMP certifies none of it.

106
00:06:41.737 --> 00:06:45.295
The obligation comes from
FISMA and Circular A-130,

107
00:06:45.295 --> 00:06:50.038
and it binds the agency whether or
not it uses a single cloud service.

108
00:06:50.038 --> 00:06:53.962
No product can write a risk tolerance
or appoint a senior official.

109
00:06:53.962 --> 00:06:56.817
These controls close when
the artifact exists,

110
00:06:56.817 --> 00:07:01.137
a named official approved it, and the
agency can show it's still followed.

111
00:07:01.137 --> 00:07:05.810
The decision belongs to the head of
agency: approve the risk strategy and its

112
00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:08.629
tolerance, because every
later plan-of-action

113
00:07:08.629 --> 00:07:11.299
date and every risk
acceptance will cite it.

114
00:07:11.299 --> 00:07:13.290
Next, contingency.

115
00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:21.076
An agency can have tested failover,
two circuits and good backups,

116
00:07:21.076 --> 00:07:24.140
and still carry an open
contingency plan finding.

117
00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:29.019
The control asks for a plan, not a
capability: who declares a contingency,

118
00:07:29.019 --> 00:07:33.815
who's told, what comes back first, and
when operations are back to normal.

119
00:07:33.815 --> 00:07:35.790
The runbooks are its appendices.

120
00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:39.523
For a Moderate system, NIST
recommends a functional exercise

121
00:07:39.523 --> 00:07:42.890
that includes a restore from
backup, not just a tabletop.

122
00:07:42.890 --> 00:07:44.689
And a replica isn't a backup;

123
00:07:44.689 --> 00:07:48.615
it copies ransomware as
faithfully as a good transaction.

124
00:07:48.615 --> 00:07:50.927
The most important number isn't technical.

125
00:07:50.927 --> 00:07:55.552
So the decision: each mission owner
signs the maximum tolerable downtime,

126
00:07:55.552 --> 00:07:59.266
and it's never lowered to fit
a convenient technical answer.

127
00:07:59.266 --> 00:08:01.160
Next, supply chain.

128
00:08:04.910 --> 00:08:08.303
Most of the code an agency runs
was written by someone else,

129
00:08:08.303 --> 00:08:10.635
and federal policy on it moved this year.

130
00:08:10.635 --> 00:08:18.135
In January, OMB's M-26-05 rescinded the
secure-development attestation mandate.

131
00:08:18.135 --> 00:08:22.010
The complete software and hardware
inventory is still required.

132
00:08:22.010 --> 00:08:26.564
Attestations and software bills of
materials became agency choices,

133
00:08:26.564 --> 00:08:28.448
made through contract terms.

134
00:08:28.448 --> 00:08:34.108
Even so, a supply chain policy, plan,
acquisition terms and notification agreements

135
00:08:34.108 --> 00:08:39.359
sit in every NIST baseline, and they
close through governance, not technology.

136
00:08:39.359 --> 00:08:43.538
From December 7, FedRAMP's
vulnerability rules bind providers,

137
00:08:43.538 --> 00:08:47.635
and the agency folds their reports
into its own plan of action.

138
00:08:47.635 --> 00:08:52.514
The decision for the contracting
officer: acquisition by acquisition,

139
00:08:52.514 --> 00:08:55.573
decide whether to require
an SBOM on request.

140
00:08:55.573 --> 00:08:57.340
Next, privacy.

141
00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:03.495
Privacy compliance is a public promise:

142
00:09:03.495 --> 00:09:08.377
why the agency holds each kind of personal
information, and under what authority.

143
00:09:08.377 --> 00:09:12.217
NIST's privacy baseline applies
whatever the impact level,

144
00:09:12.217 --> 00:09:16.056
and FedRAMP certifies none of
it, so the agency closes these

145
00:09:16.056 --> 00:09:19.816
controls end to end, through
documents the public can read.

146
00:09:19.816 --> 00:09:21.965
One timeline catches programs out.

147
00:09:21.965 --> 00:09:27.110
A new or significantly changed system of
records is reported to OMB and Congress at

148
00:09:27.110 --> 00:09:31.137
least thirty days before its notice
goes to the Federal Register,

149
00:09:31.137 --> 00:09:34.940
and new routine uses then wait
out a thirty-day comment period.

150
00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:38.084
So the decision for counsel
and the privacy official:

151
00:09:38.084 --> 00:09:42.015
start that notice at least sixty
days before the first collection.

152
00:09:42.015 --> 00:09:43.750
Next, training.

153
00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:51.275
Training is the one family
no configuration can close.

154
00:09:51.275 --> 00:09:54.078
It closes when the right
people were trained for the

155
00:09:54.078 --> 00:09:56.750
roles they actually hold,
and the record proves it.

156
00:09:56.750 --> 00:10:01.812
A dashboard showing ninety-eight percent
completion is a number with no denominator.

157
00:10:01.812 --> 00:10:03.463
Measured against which roster?

158
00:10:03.463 --> 00:10:07.287
And are the people in high-risk roles in
the two percent who haven't finished?

159
00:10:07.287 --> 00:10:10.138
The rules are simpler
than most programs think:

160
00:10:10.138 --> 00:10:13.574
training before access, and
awareness at least annually.

161
00:10:13.574 --> 00:10:17.049
Social engineering awareness
is required at Moderate;

162
00:10:17.049 --> 00:10:19.163
phishing exercises are optional.

163
00:10:19.163 --> 00:10:23.680
The decision is the CISO's: set the
completion threshold, and treat every

164
00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:28.574
overdue person in a privileged role as
a finding, whatever the percentage says.

165
00:10:28.574 --> 00:10:30.730
Next, who owns all this.

166
00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:38.713
Ask who owns compliance, and the
answer is often the security office,

167
00:10:38.713 --> 00:10:40.830
which means nobody in particular.

168
00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:45.592
The failures happen between the jobs: a
plan of action nobody updates, a privacy

169
00:10:45.592 --> 00:10:50.280
plan nobody approved, a risk acceptance
signed by someone who couldn't sign it.

170
00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:54.092
The Risk Management Framework
names an owner for every task.

171
00:10:54.092 --> 00:10:56.905
The system owner owns
more of them than anyone.

172
00:10:56.905 --> 00:11:00.793
The authorizing official is the
only one who can accept risk.

173
00:11:00.793 --> 00:11:04.543
And six roles are government-only,
so a contractor-heavy team

174
00:11:04.543 --> 00:11:08.218
can prepare every package but
sign none of those decisions.

175
00:11:08.218 --> 00:11:11.023
The decision for the
head of agency and CIO:

176
00:11:11.023 --> 00:11:15.993
make every role a signed appointment
with a name, not an organization chart.

177
00:11:15.993 --> 00:11:18.650
Next, the authorization path.

178
00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:26.302
The authorization path is where
ownership gaps show first,

179
00:11:26.302 --> 00:11:28.850
because each step hands off to the next.

180
00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:32.763
The system owner and ISSO
categorize and plan.

181
00:11:32.763 --> 00:11:36.150
The authorizing official
approves, picks the assessor,

182
00:11:36.150 --> 00:11:39.612
and approves the assessment
plan, before testing starts.

183
00:11:39.612 --> 00:11:41.124
The assessor reports.

184
00:11:41.124 --> 00:11:44.937
The system owner fixes what was
found and assembles the package.

185
00:11:44.937 --> 00:11:47.687
And the authorizing
official alone decides.

186
00:11:47.687 --> 00:11:50.206
The handoffs fail in predictable ways,

187
00:11:50.206 --> 00:11:53.512
like a system owner who
picks and pays the assessor.

188
00:11:53.512 --> 00:11:56.965
So keep the roles apart: nobody
assesses their own work,

189
00:11:56.965 --> 00:12:00.263
and the authorizing official
isn't the system owner.

190
00:12:00.263 --> 00:12:03.854
The decision: the authorizing
official selects the assessor

191
00:12:03.854 --> 00:12:07.163
and sets its independence
before the assessment starts.

192
00:12:07.163 --> 00:12:09.020
Next, the package.

193
00:12:12.770 --> 00:12:16.183
The authorizing official doesn't
read the evidence directly.

194
00:12:16.183 --> 00:12:19.338
They read a package that
summarizes it: the plans,

195
00:12:19.338 --> 00:12:22.494
the assessment, the report,
and the plan of action.

196
00:12:22.494 --> 00:12:25.437
NIST says the package may
be generated by a tool,

197
00:12:25.437 --> 00:12:29.208
and FedRAMP's agency rules
expect machine-readable records.

198
00:12:29.208 --> 00:12:34.195
Generated, the package describes the system
today, not the day someone assembled it.

199
00:12:34.195 --> 00:12:39.073
Every failing item gets one disposition:
open, with an owner and a deadline;

200
00:12:39.073 --> 00:12:44.270
an exception with an expiry date; or a
lapsed acceptance that falls back to open.

201
00:12:44.270 --> 00:12:46.370
Nothing is permanently satisfied.

202
00:12:46.370 --> 00:12:50.490
The decision for the authorizing
official: before accepting a clean

203
00:12:50.490 --> 00:12:54.682
result or an empty plan of action, ask
what the assessment didn't look at.

204
00:12:54.682 --> 00:12:56.840
Next, keeping it current.

205
00:13:00.590 --> 00:13:04.028
A package describes the system
on the day it was generated.

206
00:13:04.028 --> 00:13:06.752
Continuous monitoring
keeps that day recent.

207
00:13:06.752 --> 00:13:11.520
The program's rhythm, as its own design:
every month, regenerate the results

208
00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:16.053
and the plan of action, and report
posture to the authorizing official.

209
00:13:16.053 --> 00:13:20.139
Every quarter, review each
provider's certification report;

210
00:13:20.139 --> 00:13:22.427
FedRAMP's rule says agencies must.

211
00:13:22.427 --> 00:13:26.602
Every year, an independent assessor
re-assesses every control.

212
00:13:26.602 --> 00:13:31.290
Once monitoring is mature, a review
frequency can replace the end date.

213
00:13:31.290 --> 00:13:35.340
And vendor posture scores stay
signals, not assessment evidence.

214
00:13:35.340 --> 00:13:40.599
The decision for the CISO: name the
federal senior official who reviews each

215
00:13:40.599 --> 00:13:44.050
provider's report, and keep
a record of every review,

216
00:13:44.050 --> 00:13:47.665
before FedRAMP's rules become
mandatory on January 1.

217
00:13:47.665 --> 00:13:49.540
Next, what remains.

218
00:13:53.290 --> 00:13:57.203
Build everything the book
describes, and risk still remains.

219
00:13:57.203 --> 00:14:00.840
Self-assessment, until an
independent assessor confirms it.

220
00:14:00.840 --> 00:14:05.040
What automation can't see, like
policies and whether training worked.

221
00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:07.115
Risk inherited from providers.

222
00:14:07.115 --> 00:14:09.465
Evidence that quietly stops arriving.

223
00:14:09.465 --> 00:14:13.615
Risks outside control testing,
like mission and insider risk.

224
00:14:13.615 --> 00:14:17.265
And design not yet built, or
vendor features not yet shipped.

225
00:14:17.265 --> 00:14:19.453
None of this argues against the program.

226
00:14:19.453 --> 00:14:21.740
It argues for writing the remainder down.

227
00:14:21.740 --> 00:14:25.865
One green score hides the same risk
and makes it the official's anyway.

228
00:14:25.865 --> 00:14:31.189
So the decision: from the next update,
every package lists what's proven, asserted,

229
00:14:31.189 --> 00:14:36.190
inherited and accepted, and the authorizing
official accepts it, or doesn't.

230
00:14:36.190 --> 00:14:38.780
Finally, the decisions together.

231
00:14:42.530 --> 00:14:45.168
So, the decisions and their owners.

232
00:14:45.168 --> 00:14:46.568
None is a procurement.

233
00:14:46.568 --> 00:14:51.448
The authorizing official authorizes
the agency's own system, approves each

234
00:14:51.448 --> 00:14:54.315
categorization first,
selects the assessor,

235
00:14:54.315 --> 00:14:57.181
and decides on every
unsupported component.

236
00:14:57.181 --> 00:15:00.834
The head of agency approves
the risk tolerance and,

237
00:15:00.834 --> 00:15:03.618
with the CIO, signs named appointments.

238
00:15:03.618 --> 00:15:06.051
The CIO funds the mechanisms that produce

239
00:15:06.051 --> 00:15:08.555
records before the
tools that measure them.

240
00:15:08.555 --> 00:15:13.780
The CISO sets the training threshold and
names who reviews each provider's report.

241
00:15:13.780 --> 00:15:19.036
Mission owners sign their downtime limits,
counsel and the privacy official start

242
00:15:19.036 --> 00:15:22.235
notices early, and system
owners show what's proven,

243
00:15:22.235 --> 00:15:24.368
asserted, inherited and accepted.

244
00:15:24.368 --> 00:15:26.806
A promise is true on the day it's signed.

245
00:15:26.806 --> 00:15:28.530
Proof keeps showing itself.

246
00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:29.350
Thank you.
